## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests dynamic programming skills on a directed acyclic graph (DAG) with multiple predecessor choices and path counting for optimal solutions. The ability to handle several predecessors, implement proper tie-breaking for path counting, and correctly apply modulo arithmetic demonstrates code clarity and robustness with edge cases.

## Key Concepts
- DP on DAG with 3-step transitions (i → i+1, i+2, i+3)
- Maintaining both the optimal value and the count of optimal paths
- Modulo arithmetic (1e9+7) for path counting
- Edge-case handling for small n and negative profit values

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxProfitAndPaths(profits) {
  const n = profits.length;
  const MOD = 1000000007n;
  if (n === 0) return { best: 0, cnt: 1 };
  if (n === 1) return { best: profits[0], cnt: 1 };

  const best = new Array(n).fill(0);
  const cnt = new Array(n).fill(0);
  best[0] = profits[0];
  cnt[0] = 1;

  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    let maxVal = -Infinity;
    for (const p of [i-1, i-2, i-3]) {
      if (p >= 0) {
        const cand = best[p] + profits[i];
        if (cand > maxVal) {
          maxVal = cand;
          cnt[i] = cnt[p];
        } else if (cand === maxVal) {
          cnt[i] = (cnt[i] + cnt[p]) % MOD;
        }
      }
    }
    best[i] = maxVal;
  }
  return { best: best[n-1], cnt: cnt[n-1] };
}
```

